// The two GPU-side quad copies that replace the readback (ADR-0006, Wave D / D1).
//
// WHY THIS FILE EXISTS AT ALL. D3 got the per-chunk readback down from ~810 KB
// to 4 bytes, but the quads themselves still came back: phase 2 copied the live
// stream to system memory, the game thread unpacked and repacked it, and
// PushUpdatesToProxy uploaded the identical bytes to the pool buffer. Roughly
// 10 KB up plus 16 KB down per chunk, plus a memcpy on the game thread and
// another on the render thread, to move bytes the GPU already had into a buffer
// the GPU already owned. The 2026-07-27 line-flight legs put the cost of that
// bookkeeping at 10.1% of flight frames hitching against 0.028% with the fork
// off, render-thread-BUSY dominated with the RHI flat in both arms.
//
// These two kernels do the same two moves without leaving the GPU.
//
//   QuadCompactMain   phase 2. Copies the live prefix out of the emit pass's
//                     UPPER-BOUND buffer (MaskCount 3,072 x 32 quads = 786 KB)
//                     into one sized to the quads that actually exist (~10 KB).
//                     Nothing waits for it -- it exists so a delivered-but-not-
//                     yet-applied chunk pins its real size instead of the bound.
//
//   QuadPoolWriteMain the apply. Copies a chunk's quads into its allocated pool
//                     range AND fills the parallel chunk-id range in the same
//                     pass. The ids are what make one draw span the pool
//                     (UVoxelGpuPoolComponent), and writing them here rather
//                     than through a lock is what lets the CPU stop touching
//                     the geometry entirely.
//
// WHY BOTH ARE COMPUTE PASSES AND NOT AddCopyBufferPass. Two reasons, and the
// second is the one that would have cost a day:
//
//   1. The pool write has to fill OutChunkIds as well. A constant store in a
//      pass that is already running is free; a second mechanism for it is not,
//      and a CPU-side id upload would put the render-thread lock back.
//   2. FD3D12CommandContext::RHICopyBufferRegion asserts
//      `pSourceResource != pDestResource` (D3D12Buffer.cpp:970). Two small
//      RDG-pooled buffers can legitimately be suballocated from ONE D3D12
//      resource, so the compaction copy could trip that assert in a checked
//      build depending on nothing more than pool state. A compute pass has no
//      such hazard.
//
// WHY IT IS NOT IN worldgen.ush. Same two reasons as VoxelQuadScan.usf, which
// is the precedent this file follows: the determinism digest compiles
// worldgen.ush two ways and pins a hash over the result, and neither of these
// kernels has any business inside that contract -- so living here means they
// cannot move the digest, cannot require a SPIR-V respin, and cannot fail the
// shader lint for reasons unrelated to world generation. worldgen.ush is also
// the file ground rule 10 is about: it is shared with the main checkout that
// other waves run editors out of.
//
// QUAD FORMAT. A quad is a uint2 and is COPIED, never interpreted. The layout
// is PackVoxelChunkQuad's (VoxelMeshTypes.h) and is a contract with
// DecodeVoxelQuadVertex in VoxelQuadDecode.ush; voxel.GPU.VerifyAsyncMesh has
// measured the emit pass byte-identical to MeshChunkBricks +
// PackVoxelChunkQuad. Nothing here needs to know any of that, and deliberately
// does not: a copy that understood the format could get it wrong.

#include "/Engine/Public/Platform.ush"

StructuredBuffer<uint2>   InQuads;
RWStructuredBuffer<uint2> OutQuads;
RWStructuredBuffer<uint>  OutChunkIds;

// First source element belonging to this chunk. The emit pass writes at
// QuadWriteBase, which is 0 on every path today, and QuadCompactMain rebases to
// 0 -- so this is 0 in practice both times. It is threaded through anyway
// rather than assumed, because the failure mode of assuming it is a copy that
// starts at the wrong offset and produces geometry that is plausible, self-
// consistent, and somebody else's.
uint SrcFirst;
// First slot of the chunk's pool allocation. Unused by QuadCompactMain, which
// always writes a fresh buffer from 0.
uint DstFirst;
// How many quads are live. Both kernels dispatch exactly this many threads;
// the bound is restated in the shader because a dispatch size is not a
// guarantee (the group size rounds up).
uint NumQuads;
// The chunk-table entry these quads belong to. Pool-write only.
uint ChunkId;

// One thread per live quad. 64 to match the wave-agnostic group size every
// other kernel in this project uses -- nothing here reads a lane index or a
// wave width, so the choice is arbitrary and must stay that way.
[numthreads(64, 1, 1)]
void QuadCompactMain(uint3 tid : SV_DispatchThreadID)
{
	const uint i = tid.x;
	// The group size rounds up, so the tail threads of the last group are real
	// and must not write. This is also the bound UNGUARDED_WRITE wants to see.
	if (i >= NumQuads)
	{
		return;
	}
	OutQuads[i] = InQuads[SrcFirst + i];
}

[numthreads(64, 1, 1)]
void QuadPoolWriteMain(uint3 tid : SV_DispatchThreadID)
{
	const uint i = tid.x;
	if (i >= NumQuads)
	{
		return;
	}
	// The two writes are the whole point of one pass rather than two: a quad
	// and the id naming its owning chunk are written together, so there is no
	// window in which the pool holds geometry that belongs to nobody. The
	// allocation is exactly NumQuads slots (UVoxelGpuPoolComponent::AddChunkFromGpu
	// calls Pool.Alloc with this count), so there is no tail to hide here --
	// unlike the CPU UpdateChunk path, which reuses an oversized slot and points
	// the leftovers at the hidden chunk.
	OutQuads   [DstFirst + i] = InQuads[SrcFirst + i];
	OutChunkIds[DstFirst + i] = ChunkId;
}
